diff --git a/specs/dependency-recovery-safety.md b/specs/dependency-recovery-safety.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5b06320
--- /dev/null
+++ b/specs/dependency-recovery-safety.md
@@ -0,0 +1,534 @@
+# Dependency recovery safety and hidden tracked deletions
+
+Status: draft for independent review and owner decision. No production activation.
+Baseline: `34d6030c61f3f02783fd9bd096ff1fd7cc39f8a8`.
+
+## 1. Scope and authority
+
+CON-03, SEC-05, EVD-03 and FC-09 already require preserving human work,
+reconciling uncertain effects and invalidating stale evidence. Tech Spec
+sections 7.4 and 9.11 describe the existing journal and dependency workflow.
+The C1-C3 candidates do not change budgets, schema versions, commands or consent.
+Section 7 proposes new versioned records and a decision route; it activates none.
+The owner's direction authorizes drafting, review and isolated characterization,
+not the new policy decision below or an implementation exceeding fixed limits.
+
+The old source candidate `f1-recovery-source-candidate.patch` is rejected as a
+safe-rollback implementation. A post-resolver read can contain human edits.
+Matching it cannot prove resolver ownership; checking hashes before separate
+writes also permits a human write between the check and replacement.
+
+## 2. Existing-contract repairs
+
+### C1. Crash recovery never restores dependency files
+
+The baseline `dependency()` handler in `recover-effects.ts` unconditionally
+calls `restoreFiles` today, without checking current contents or complete
+snapshot availability. C1 replaces this destructive branch; it is not merely
+an additional guard on an otherwise safe rollback.
+
+After the existing process-ownership reconciler establishes that the job is no
+longer running, dependency resolution recovery validates both saved pre-images
+and compares both current files before recording a result. A null pre-image means prior absence,
+not an empty file. Missing blobs and unreadable/excluded/linked/nonregular
+current paths are unresolved, not absence.
+
+If both current contents equal both validated pre-images, record
+`completed: false` and explain that the files currently match their pre-images.
+This does not assert that a resolver never ran or that external effects never
+happened. Otherwise append no result, perform no project write or removal, and
+report uncertainty. Preserve the unresolved journal and fence dependent work
+through existing recovery mechanisms. `stop` remains process-only.
+
+No expected resolver post-image exists in the schema-1 intent; recovery must
+not reconstruct one from current files. Equality observations are not a
+cross-file snapshot and never authorize a later project write.
+
+### C2. Snapshot restoration must validate the complete read set first
+
+For any remaining restore caller, resolve every non-null saved blob before
+the first project mutation. An unavailable blob must produce an explicit
+failure with no writes/removals. A valid empty blob remains valid empty content.
+This repairs partial restoration and empty-string substitution only; it does
+not establish ownership or solve the check/write race. Until D1 is settled,
+normal resolver rollback remains unsafe even with this repair.
+
+### C3. Hidden tracked deletions must change candidate identity
+
+For each indexed path Git hides with assume-unchanged or skip-worktree, read
+its actual admissible content. For a proven missing path, remove the entry
+from inventory and emit one unstaged `.D` row with empty work blob and the
+original index mode/blob. Never change the real index or index flags.
+Use no-follow parent admission; a linked or unreadable parent is not proof of
+deletion. Inability to establish a safe path must explicitly refuse inventory,
+not return a clean candidate or silently label deletion.
+
+Reuse `hiddenChanges`, the existing entry/status shapes, digest and downstream
+freshness binding. No new inventory or deletion representation is needed.
+Excluded paths retain their existing policy behavior. Ordinary tracked
+deletions and hidden modifications remain unchanged. Candidate freshness is
+an observation, not a filesystem-wide atomic snapshot guarantee.
+
+## 3. D1: replacement normal-resolution contract (owner decision)
+
+The current section 9.11 step 5 promises conditional automatic restoration.
+Its ownership premise is not established by the current adapters. Neither a
+kernel lock (which humans do not honor), a package-manager exit status, nor
+inode/hash comparison followed by rename supplies a compare-and-swap over
+the two human-editable files.
+
+| Alternative | Behavior | Consequence |
+|---|---|---|
+| A. Preserve and fence (recommended) | Remove automatic restoration on resolver failure, policy rejection, decline and install failure. Keep snapshots and changed files; do not install or grant authority on a rejected/failed operation. Report unresolved dependency edits until separately reconciled. | Smallest honest safety policy; changes step 5 and decline UX. Requires an explicit reconciliation contract, not an invented answer flag. |
+| B. Resolve privately, propose changes | Run the manager against an isolated copy with admitted inputs, no live manifest/lock writes. Show the proposed diff; publication still needs a qualified concurrent-writer protocol or user-managed application. | Better future UX, but registry configuration, workspaces, relative paths and publication require a separate spec and capacity review. A scratch directory alone does not solve live publication. |
+| C. Prove exclusive writer control | Provide a qualified mechanism excluding arbitrary human writers across both paths during publication/restoration. | Current ports and ordinary filesystem rename do not provide this. Advisory locks, post-read hashes and last-moment checks do not qualify. |
+
+Recommendation: choose A as the safety floor, with no promise of automatic
+rollback. The exact unresolved-to-resolved transition is a second decision:
+retain the operation's saved references and attribution until the user has
+manually repaired/reviewed the files; re-read actual contents, re-run policy
+and verification, and reacquire any needed authority before a fresh install.
+Do not silently clear the pending operation and call the files restored.
+No new command, question option, stored record or reason code is approved by
+this text. The concrete decoder/retention/fence-release protocol needs review
+and owner sign-off before A's normal-flow implementation.
+
+C1-C3 follow existing safety requirements; D1 changes an explicit spec promise.
+Authorizing requirement closure is not approval of A's unspecified transition.
+The implementation decision is therefore irreducibly: approve preservation
+instead of automatic rollback, then sign off its concrete reconciliation
+transition. Existing preview/accept/install behavior must not be bypassed.
+
+## 4. Distinguishing examples and tests
+
+| Example | Required observation | Test file |
+|---|---|---|
+| Crash before resolver writes, both pre-images intact | No file writes; not-performed result describes current equality, not external history | `packages/kernel/test/dependency-recovery-safety.test.ts` (direct handler); `dependency.test.ts` (pipeline) |
