+## 7. D1A concrete proposed reconciliation protocol
+
+This section is a proposal for a single owner sign-off, not an available
+command. It replaces the unspecified second decision in section 3 with the
+complete transition below. D1 and protocol-version/capacity approval remain
+required; no normal-flow implementation is included in this lane.
+
+### 7.1 Records and compatibility
+
+Current schema-1 `work.dependency` has specs, urgent, quote, scripts, manager,
+before blob references and after hashes. The unresolved dependency journal
+intent has work, phase, manager, before and optional scripts; no resolver-owned
+post-image or source writer identity is established. Preserve legacy decoding
+and C1 behavior; do not reinterpret existing `completed: false` as preserved
+abandonment.
+
+Propose the following strict records under schema 3 for both state and journal,
+coordinated with migration. Do not silently add an effect/outcome
+to the frozen schema-1 or schema-2 effect inventories. Its exact activation
+version is 3 in this proposal, not an instruction to activate schema 2 or 3.
+Older writers must fail admission before mutation. The coupled migration work
+must preserve schema-1 decoding/upcast unchanged; format conversion never
+invents this decision, authority, counters, receipts or timestamps.
+
+- New decision kind: `dependency-reconcile`.
+- New journal effect: `dependency-reconcile`; ID from the kernel random port.
+- New `SETTLE["dependency-reconcile"]` registry entry implementing section 7.3.
+- Intent `op`: `{work, generation, targets, manager, before, observed,
+  request, policyHash, binding, action: "abandon-preserve", quote}`.
+- `request`: `{specs, urgent, scripts, quote}` copied from the original durable
+  preview when present; otherwise null, explicitly meaning that legacy
+  resolution intent did not retain it. New schema-3 resolution intents retain
+  this request too (scripts initially empty, never fabricated from live files).
+- `targets`: an array containing the same work's single unresolved dependency
+  resolution intent ID; multiple/conflicting intents are refused, not merged.
+  The manager and before references must match that exact intent. An intact
+  pending preview uses an empty targets list and identifies its revision
+  through the binding. Install-phase uncertainty is ineligible.
+- `before`: existing `{manifest: blob|null, lock: blob|null}` references.
+  References stay even when unavailable. No fabricated replacement blob.
+- `observed`: `{manifest: digest|null, lock: digest|null}`; digest is SHA-256
+  of safely read current bytes, null is proven absence. Unsafe reads refuse
+  this decision, because the card cannot accurately identify reviewed files.
+- `binding`: hash of workspace ID, work ID/generation, exact target intent
+  payloads, manager, before, observed, policyHash, and current pending
+  dependency preview identity (or null). Policy hash covers the effective
+  dependency policy, exclusions, protected paths and relevant command inputs.
+- Result of each original target dependency intent: `{status:
+  "abandoned-preserved", reconciliation: id}`. This is a new discriminated
+  terminal outcome, never `completed: false` or a claim rollback succeeded.
+- Reconciliation result: `{completed: true, binding}`. Completion means only
+  this record-only settlement occurred, not file recovery or installation.
+- New optional per-work `dependencyReconciliation`: `{id, binding, manager,
+  before, observed, targets, quote, settledAt, status: "abandoned-preserved"}`.
+  One current record is sufficient; earlier settlements remain in the journal.
+  No new grants, receipts, budgets, accepted answers or vetted paths arise.
+
+When failure/rejection/decline occurs normally under D1A, use this same
+record-only settlement preparation, rather than restore files or silently
+drop the pending preview. Preserve the existing before refs and original
+specs/quote in the journal intent. The UI offers reconciliation; it must
+not say the files are restored. An install error remains separate uncertain
+external execution and is not closable by this manifest-only route.
+Specifically replace the restore calls in `dependency.ts`'s `decline` and
+`dependency-start.ts`'s `afterResolve` resolver-error/policy-block branches.
+Failure or policy rejection leaves the resolution intent unresolved and
+returns its real failure plus a reconciliation next action; it must not append
+`refused()`'s present `completed: false` result. A normal preview decline
+keeps that exact preview until explicit settlement. C1 may still safely
+settle an unchanged-beforeimage resolution as not performed on the next
+recovery; that case needs no fence release and offers no reconciliation card.
+The new route accepts no-target only with an intact pending preview, never
+as a free-standing ability to discard arbitrary uncertainty.
+
+### 7.2 Narrow dispatch and preparation
+
+`decide {kind: "dependency-reconcile", h}` previews only the named work.
+It is the sole new exception to the global recovery fence. It may inspect
+only target resolution intents and prepare/settle this record-only action.
+All other uncertainty (unknown effects, accounting, orphan/liveness ambiguity,
+installation/scripts, incompatible state, another work's effects) still
+refuses before question or result. No generic "ignore recovery" flag exists.
+
+Compatibility and adopted policy admission, verified accounting, the current
+work handle/generation, ownership and current revision checks still apply.
+The existing process reconciler must establish termination for every target
+job; unknown or live PID/start-time ownership refuses. No counter is reset,
+refund issued, evidence reused or process terminated by answering this card.
+The `stop` route remains available independently.
+
+The kernel safely observes both paths and presents exact refs/hashes,
+operation IDs, missing snapshots and uncertainty, without exposing file bodies.
+In `pipeline-mutate.ts`, `planInLock` must make this narrowly typed exception
+at its pre-dispatch `recovered.uncertain` gate, not in the unreachable decision
+handler. Extend internal recovery evidence with structured unresolved entries
+`{effect, id, work, message}` and an explicit uncorrelated-blocker list; keep
+the public reason/effect rendering compatible. Only exact resolution target
+entries or verified replay of their reconciliation may pass. Missing
+correlation fails closed; arbitrary message text is never an admission key.
+`dependency-reconcile` is not an `EFFECT_DECISIONS` entry and acquires no
+source editing lease: "ownership" here means work/process identity, not
+permission to overwrite project files.
+The question ID is `dependency-reconcile`; its single affirmative answer is
+`abandon-preserve`, with `cancel` as the non-settling answer. The card states:
+"Leave package.json and the lockfile exactly as they are and abandon this
+dependency operation. This does not verify their contents, restore files,
+approve installation, or undo external side effects."
+The question binding must be supplied with the answer, not reconstructed from
+the latest state merely because the same answer text was repeated.
+
+### 7.3 Answer, publication and crash behavior
+
+On `decide {kind: "dependency-reconcile", h, answers:
+{"dependency-reconcile": "abandon-preserve"}, propose: {binding}, quote}`,
+recompute every binding input after ordinary admission under the kernel lock.
+A stale handle, replaced target, different current bytes/policy or expired
+preview refuses with existing revision/freshness error semantics and requires
+a newly displayed card. The user's own nonempty quote records their decision;
+source contents are not inferred as consent. No generic grant action is added.
+
+No project write/removal occurs before, during or after settlement. A human
+edit after observation therefore survives; the stored observed hashes are
+historical, not current facts in the response. The response recomputes live
+candidate identity or explicitly reports it moved and requires fresh checks.
+It must not claim equality with the approved snapshot after concurrent edits.
+
+Publication order under the transaction lock:
+1. Fsync reconciliation intent with its exact frozen binding and authority.
+2. Fsync each target's `abandoned-preserved` result referencing that intent.
+3. Publish state clearing only the exact pending dependency preview/answers,
+   retaining `dependencyReconciliation` and existing touched/includeDirty/
+   keepOut records unchanged. Do not add resolver ownership or grants.
+4. Fsync reconciliation result. Ordinary accounting/cached response publication
+   follows existing revision rules; cache reuse requires live fact identity.
+
+The typed recovery handler replays a durable reconciliation intent idempotently
+without touching project files. It finishes missing target results only while
+they reference the same exact intents; contradictory terminal records remain
+unresolved. Its successor state compares predecessor preview/generation/binding:
+already applied means no-op; an absent superseded preview cannot be recreated;
+an unrelated newer preview is never cleared. On backup fallback, verified
+accounting and no-conflict checks still gate replay; missing work is retained
+as unresolved rather than recreated with authority.
+`settledAt` is the frozen reconciliation intent envelope time, never the
+replay clock; repeating recovery cannot change the approved disposition.
+
+A crash at each boundary may leave a partially settled target list. Remaining
+unresolved reconciliation intent keeps the fence until replay completes; an
+original target already closed does not authorize ordinary dispatch before
+replay. Any write/journal/read failure keeps uncertainty, with snapshots and
+state intact to the last durable revision. A late human edit cannot invalidate
+the already authorized record-only abandonment: no source rollback occurs.
+
+The old operation-specific fence is released only after all exact target
+results and successor state are durable with the matching reconciliation
+result; other uncertainty remains fenced. This action does not release a
+generic ownership/accounting or incompatible-format fence.
+
+### 7.4 After settlement; cancellation and retention
+
+Both manifest/lock paths remain unexplained/unverified unless existing
+attribution and independent evidence already justify them. No new Tenet
+`touched` claim is created from the resolution's observed hashes. Existing
+scope/dirty-overlap, protected-path and dependency-unverified rules still
+apply. A later source digest change invalidates prior receipts normally.
+
+No direct continuation to `npm ci` follows reconciliation. A new dependency
+request requires a fresh quote/authority, safe current snapshot, normal
+policy verification, new preview and new install consent; it cannot reuse
+the abandoned preview, answer or failed resolver evidence. Approval of this
+card is not a permanent allowance for the package or its scripts.
+
+`cancel` or a dismissed card appends no terminal effect result, keeps the
+operation/preview and snapshot references, and leaves dependent work fenced.
+It does not delete files, abandon work, stop an active process, or clear
+grants. An explicit `stop` remains the process action. Deleting a work record
+must not remove the journal fence or the retained snapshot references.
+
+Keep all before blobs referenced by unresolved dependency/reconciliation
+intents, the pending preview or the current reconciliation record. Existing
+retention may prune superseded terminal records only with a durable reduction
+preserving their disposition and without dropping still referenced blobs.
+No automatic private backup is exposed or copied into a project path. User
+forgetting/retention rights and bounded blob retention must be reviewed with
+the storage protocol; missing content stays honestly missing rather than
+blocking record-only acknowledgement or synthesizing content.
+
+### 7.5 Additional acceptance controls
+
+New `dependency-reconciliation.test.ts` must cover exact same-work resolution
+admission, conflicting/missing targets, unavailable preimages with safe live
+reads, unsafe live reads, orphan/live process and incomplete accounting
+refusal, explicit quote and stale answer/work/policy/candidate bindings,
+cancel preserving the fence, no filesystem mutation, no grants or ownership
+created, fresh policy/preview/install consent, and dirty/foreign attribution.
+Real-process barrier controls interleave human writes before observation,
+after observation and through every publication step. Kill after each
+target result and state/result publication; resume/retry/fallback twice and
+assert one disposition, preserved human bytes and no authority resurrection.
+Old readers/writers must refuse the proposed format and decoder tests must
+reject malformed disposition/target/binding shapes. These are proposed
+tests, not an implemented interface or a qualification result.
